  "reasoning": "The patient presents with fever, weakness, and distinct sores on the face that are itchy, painful, and leak a yellow ooze. These specific dermatological symptoms—particularly facial sores exuding yellow fluid—are classic hallmarks of impetigo, a superficial bacterial skin infection. While chickenpox also involves fever and sores, it typically presents as a widespread vesicular rash across the body rather than localized facial lesions with yellow exudate. Reference cases confirm that facial sores oozing yellow or rust-colored liquid are diagnosed as impetigo. Therefore, the clinical picture aligns precisely with impetigo.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]impetigo[/DIAGNOSIS]"
